
.TH TIMEDEXEC 1 "November 2026" "CSC 332" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timedexec \- execute a program with resource limits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timedexec
.RB [ \-t " seconds" ]
.RB [ \-c " seconds" ]
.RB [ \-m " MB" ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.B --
.I command
.RI [ arguments... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timedexec
executes a Linux command while enforcing specified resource
limits. It supports wall-clock timeouts, CPU-time limits,
and memory address-space limits.

The command uses fork() and execvp() to execute programs,
setrlimit() to enforce CPU and memory limits, and wait4()
to monitor child processes and collect resource usage.

At least one resource limit must be specified.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-t " seconds"
Maximum wall-clock execution time in seconds.
.TP
.BI \-c " seconds"
Maximum CPU time in seconds.
.TP
.BI \-m " MB"
Maximum memory address space in megabytes.
.TP
.B \-v
Print process ID, configured limits, and elapsed wall time.
.TP
.B \-s
Print user/system CPU time and peak resident memory (Linux KiB).
.TP
.B \-h
Display help information.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B timedexec -t 3 -- sleep 10
Terminates sleep after approximately three seconds.
.TP
.B timedexec -c 2 -- yes
Limits CPU usage to two seconds.
.TP
.B timedexec -m 50 -- program
Runs a program with a 50 MB address-space limit.
.TP
.B timedexec -t 5 -c 2 -m 100 -- program
Combines wall-clock, CPU, and memory limits.
.TP
.B timedexec -v -s -t 5 -- sleep 1
Shows execution details and actual resource consumption.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Command completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments or a system error.
.TP
.B 124
Wall-clock timeout exceeded.
.TP
.B 127
Requested command could not be executed.
.TP
.B 128+N
Command terminated by signal N.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGINT and SIGTERM are handled to interrupt execution
and clean up the child process.
